.TH pdfpagefacts 1 "16 November 2026"
.SH NAME
pdfpagefacts \- page facts of a PDF as JSON lines, for routing pages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pdfpagefacts
[options]
.RI [ PDF-file | \- ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pdfpagefacts
reads a PDF (a path, or standard input for
.BR \- )
and writes its page facts to standard output, one JSON object per line (schema
.IR doppler-page-facts/1 ):
one doc line, one line per page in ascending order, and a run line. It runs the text
extraction pass without laying out lines, writes no text, reads no image data and renders
nothing.
.PP
Facts describe each page's geometry, its text layer (characters and words, visible and
invisible, coverage), images (count, coverage, scan detection, and per image the codec,
bits, colour space, masks, copy class, the visible and invisible words inside it (whole box,
and by word centre) and its encoded bytes per pixel, from the stream's declared length when it is consistent, never
decoded), fonts, odd characters, vector drawing (paths, rules, a grid score, the ruled grids pdftomarkdown's table
search starts from with their boxes, rows and columns and, per grid, its distinct thin
horizontal and vertical rule lines and how many of its four sides carry one, shadings, patterns, clips, figure
clusters), math and table encodings (the
.B mathtables
family, below), form fields, annotations
and optional content, plus document-level facts (version, encryption, xref state,
structure, forms, layers) and a stable vocabulary of
.I unusualFeatures
and
.IR parseWarnings .
Every field and value is listed in docslurp/page-facts/README.md; the JSON Schema is
docslurp/page-facts/doppler-page-facts-1.schema.json.
.PP
.B "pdftotext \-page\-facts\-fd"
and
.B "pdftomarkdown \-\-page\-facts"
compute the same facts on their own pass: for the same input, range, level and include list
the three write byte-identical lines (except where pdftotext reads more: \-glyphs, or a
rebuild by its XHTML header's document information reads). Without
.B \-timing
the output is the same on every run, and a page's line does not depend on which other pages
were processed.
.PP
The output is ASCII; a field that was not computed is absent, never 0; lists and strings are
capped; numbers are finite and ratios are within 0 and 1. No document text, path, URI, field
name or value is written, except the capped and sanitised
.I producer
and
.I creator
strings, which are untrusted. Diagnostics go to standard error as fixed strings; Poppler's
own messages are dropped, and standard error points at /dev/null while the document is
processed. Nothing is written but standard output, and descriptors above 2 are closed at
start. Once the file is open, and before it is parsed, the process confines itself (see
.BR \-confine ).
Run it under a memory limit and a timeout, inside the sandbox customer files require: it
parses hostile input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " number"
First page (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-l " number"
Last page (default the last page).
.TP
.BI \-level " basic|full"
basic (the default): geometry, text counts, layer and coverage, image totals and scan
detection, parse warnings and features; full: every family, mathtables included (about 7%
more CPU than full without it on textbook pages).
.TP
.BI \-include " list"
Families added to basic, comma-separated: fonts, oddtext, images-detail (per-image items,
words inside and centred inside, bytes per pixel), vectors (including ruled grids and their
thin-rule counts), forms, annots, ocg,
structure, mathtables (the packed math and table words below; with the other families, their
readable fields). Basic alone does not include mathtables;
.B "\-include mathtables"
at basic adds only three integers a page.
.TP
.BI \-opw " password"
Owner password.
.TP
.BI \-upw " password"
User password.
.TP
.BI \-max\-pages " number"
Stop after this many pages, writing the run line with
.I stop
and exiting with 3 (default 100000).
.TP
.BI \-max\-memory " MiB"
Address-space limit, 0 for none (default 4096). Running out of memory exits with 3.
.TP
.BI \-scan\-max\-words " number"
A page with more visible words is not a scan (default 20).
.TP
.BI \-scan\-min\-coverage " share"
The share of the page one image, or the images together, must cover for a scan, in (0,1]
(default 0.85).
.TP
.BI \-scan\-layer\-overlap " share"
The share of the page a second image overlapping the scan must cover to make it layered, in
(0,1] (default 0.5).
.IP
A share is a plain decimal number (0.8, .8, 1); anything else is a usage error. When any of
the three \-scan options is not its default, the page facts doc line records all three
.RI ( scanThresholds );
with the defaults the output does not change.
.TP
.BI \-confine " require|auto|off"
After opening the file and before parsing it, drop the ability to write, create or remove
files, use the network or run programs (Landlock and seccomp on Linux). require (the
default) exits with 5 when it cannot confine; auto applies what the kernel supports and
writes a warning when that is not everything; off does nothing.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " seconds"
Wall-clock limit for the whole run, 0 for none (default 600). Exceeding it exits with 3; the
lines already written are whole.
.TP
.B \-timing
Add factsMs to page lines and totalMs and peakRssKiB to the run line. These differ between
runs.
.TP
.B \-v
Print copyright and version information.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage information.
.RB ( \-help
and
.B \-\-help
are equivalent.)
.SH MATH AND TABLE ENCODINGS
With the
.B mathtables
family each page line carries three integers, one fact per route the router tells apart
(never one score); the bit order is an API and bits are only appended.
.PP
.I mathBits
(bit 0 first): text-math (math-font characters with Unicode), stacked-math (fraction bars or
vertically stacked math glyphs), actualtext-tex (marked-content ActualText holding TeX),
alt-tex (reserved), mathml (reserved), raster-strips (short wide images in the text flow),
glyph-outlines (small curve-dense drawing in the text flow), type3-bitmap-font,
math-font-no-unicode, plain-unicode-math (scripts, operators or math alphanumerics in text
fonts), formula-struct (reserved), math-matrix (a grid of math glyphs), analysis-truncated.
.PP
.I tableBits
(bit 0 first): rule-grid, hrule-open, booktabs, whitespace (gutter-aligned rows, no rules),
shaded-rows, tagged-table (reserved), raster-table-like (an image shape and codec, never decoded),
outlined-text-grid, math-grid (a grid of math glyphs: not a table route),
analysis-truncated. Reserved bits needed the structure tree, which is never read; they
keep their positions and are never set.
.PP
.I encodingCounts
packs 13 four-bit log2 buckets (0 for 0, else floor(log2 n)+1, at most 15; nibble i at bits
4i to 4i+3): math-font characters with Unicode, plain Unicode math characters, unreadable math
glyphs, math strips, glyph-outline clusters, reserved (0), TeX sources (marked-content
ActualText), reserved (0), ruled grids, rule tables, whitespace tables, reserved (0), math
regions.
.PP
At
.B full
the readable fields come too: text.unicodeMath, fonts.mathClasses (glyphs by a fixed font-class
vocabulary: texType1, otfMath, officeMath, webMath, symbol, type3Bitmap, text; never a name),
images.items[].stripLike and inTextFlow, images.mathStrips, vectors.glyphOutlineClusters,
vectors.ruleTables (horizontal-rule tables, booktabs or open, at most 64), the page's
structure object (marked-content ActualText spans and the TeX-like ones), mathRegions (text, outline and raster boxes, at most 64), mathLayout, tableLayout,
mathFlags and tableFlags (the bits as names). docslurp/page-facts/README.md has the exact
rules and bounds. These facts read a Type 3 font's first 8 glyph procedures (64 KB each), the
first 4 KB of an embedded font program, without rebuilding the cross-reference table, and
never the structure tree.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
0
Facts written.
.TP
1
The document cannot be opened (a doc line with
.I error
is written), has no pages, or standard output cannot be written.
.TP
2
The document is encrypted and needs a password (a doc line with
.I locked
is written).
.TP
3
A limit was reached (\-max\-pages, after the lines; memory; or \-timeout).
.TP
5
.B \-confine require
could not confine.
.TP
99
Usage error, including a first page past the last.
.PP
Treat any other status (a signal, say) as failure. A missing run line means the run was cut
short.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pdftotext (1),
.BR pdfpagecount (1),
.BR pdfregions (1)
